WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.114
They always say that
"At Google, you're working with

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.074
a lot of the best and the brightest."
I think that is true.

00:00:06.724 --> 00:00:08.130
My name is, Kourtni Marshall.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.393
I'm an application engineer
at Corp Eng in Google.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.910
Corp Eng is basically the IT of Google.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:22.733
And our main mission
is to keep Google productive internally.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.650
We create applications
mainly for my fellow Googlers

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.463
that solve everyday
problems that we might have.

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.440
The thing that I love
about being an engineer

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.680
is figuring out how to
solve these problems,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.350
like, there's a problem that we have.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.430
And, right now, it's going unanswered.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.130
But, with the work that
myself and others do,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.734
we can make that problem go away.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.790
My father had dropped out of high school.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.100
My brother had dropped out of high school,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.270
and I was on the path of like,

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.220
eh, maybe I'll drop out of high school.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.110
The only reason I'm even here
is because a friend of mine

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:05.360
in high school literally saw
me checking out a little bit,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.947
and said, "Hey, here's some resources

00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:09.110
to get you back on track."

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.450
So, I have to recognize
that without people looking out for me,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.350
I probably wouldn't be where I'm at today.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.330
And I was also blessed because my father,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.680
he ended up getting us a old computer.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.580
But, we were like one of the
only people on the block

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.190
that had a computer in their house.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.060
I was seven years old, and
I remember the first thing

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.550
I really learned on it was
a language called Basic.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.466
And, all I could figure out how to do,

00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:34.520
I'd read the things,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.570
I could figure out how to
make what was called a Sprite,

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:38.980
move across the screen.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.780
And I was like, wow, this is awesome!

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.020
So, I think that's one of the
things that actually moved me

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.223
toward being interested
in computers at all.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.720
To be successful at Google,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.510
you have to have a drive to learn.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.810
There's so many things to learn
within the company itself.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.210
And there's so many opportunities, really,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.240
to have an impact within the company.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.090
Especially if you have that
desire to find those things

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.270
that you really are most passionate about,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.120
and then kinda do deep
dives into those things.
